Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** — it hosts your app in-process with an in-memory `TestServer`, so you get real HTTP without opening a port.

### Setup

```csharp
// NuGet: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing, xunit
public class McpTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public McpTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }
}
```

### Example: Test MCP `initialize` handshake

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Initialize_ReturnsServerCapabilities()
{
    var request = new
    {
        jsonrpc = "2.0",
        id = 1,
        method = "initialize",
        @params = new
        {
            protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
            capabilities = new { },
            clientInfo = new { name = "test-client", version = "1.0" }
        }
    };

    var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);

    response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
    var body = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
    Assert.Equal("2.0", body.GetProperty("jsonrpc").GetString());
    Assert.Equal(1, body.GetProperty("id").GetInt32());

    var result = body.GetProperty("result");
    Assert.Equal("2025-03-26", result.GetProperty("protocolVersion").GetString());
    Assert.True(result.TryGetProperty("capabilities", out _));
    Assert.True(result.TryGetProperty("serverInfo", out _));
}
```

### Example: Test `tools/list` and `tools/call`

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task ToolsCall_ReturnsExpectedResult()
{
    // List tools
    var listReq = new { jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 2, method = "tools/list" };
    var listResp = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", listReq);
    var listBody = await listResp.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
    var tools = listBody.GetProperty("result").GetProperty("tools");
    Assert.True(tools.GetArrayLength() > 0);

    // Call a tool
    var callReq = new
    {
        jsonrpc = "2.0",
        id = 3,
        method = "tools/call",
        @params = new
        {
            name = tools[0].GetProperty("name").GetString(),
            arguments = new { /* tool-specific args */ }
        }
    };
    var callResp = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", callReq);
    callResp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

    var callBody = await callResp.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<JsonElement>();
    var content = callBody.GetProperty("result").GetProperty("content");
    Assert.True(content.GetArrayLength() > 0);
}
```

### Key points

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **No real port** | `WebApplicationFactory` uses an in-memory `TestServer` |
| **Swap dependencies** | Override `ConfigureWebHost` to replace DB/services with fakes |
| **SSE transport** | Use `factory.CreateDefaultClient()` + read the stream with `ReadAsStreamAsync` |
| **Auth** | Add test auth via `.AddAuthentication("Test")` in the factory override |
| **Endpoint path** | Adjust `"/mcp"` to match your actual route |

### Overriding services (e.g., mock a database)

```csharp
public class CustomFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>
{
    protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.ConfigureServices(services =>
        {
            // Replace a real service with a fake
            services.RemoveAll<IMyRepository>();
            services.AddSingleton<IMyRepository, FakeRepository>();
        });
    }
}
```

This gives you full MCP protocol coverage — initialize, tool calls, resource reads, error codes — all in-process with fast, deterministic tests.
